X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:127826

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.502
In this lesson, you'll learn what Semantic Caching is,

2
00:00:02.502 --> 00:00:05.772
why it matters and how it helps AI Agents reuse results

3
00:00:05.772 --> 00:00:07.774
to reduce cost and latency.

4
00:00:07.874 --> 00:00:10.377
Let's dive in. Let's start at the beginning.

5
00:00:10.477 --> 00:00:13.680
Model quality tends to go up with cost per token.

6
00:00:13.713 --> 00:00:17.083
That curve is improving, but the price and latency tradeoff

7
00:00:17.083 --> 00:00:19.886
is still the gating factor on real-world deployments.

8
00:00:20.286 --> 00:00:23.490
This is a real tension, because popular models for reasoning

9
00:00:23.490 --> 00:00:26.493
and solving complex tasks like GPT-5 or Claude

10
00:00:26.493 --> 00:00:28.895
are just more expensive and also slower.

11
00:00:29.262 --> 00:00:32.532
Here we see some results produced by artificialanalysis.ai.

12
00:00:32.832 --> 00:00:36.136
In their comparison of multiple foundation models against one another,

13
00:00:36.236 --> 00:00:38.838
and we compare intelligence versus price.

14
00:00:39.139 --> 00:00:41.841
So for many teams, inference, not data plumbing,

15
00:00:42.142 --> 00:00:44.544
is now the dominant unit cost in AI systems.

16
00:00:44.778 --> 00:00:48.982
organizations are building Retrieval Augmented Generation systems or RAG for short.

17
00:00:49.382 --> 00:00:51.818
These systems can infuse domain specific knowledge

18
00:00:51.818 --> 00:00:54.587
into the prompt of a large language model

19
00:00:54.587 --> 00:00:57.057
and make them so they reduce hallucinations by

20
00:00:57.057 --> 00:00:59.492
inserting the right information at the right time.

21
00:00:59.826 --> 00:01:02.896
and also stay fresh by providing up-to-date information

22
00:01:02.896 --> 00:01:06.199
at runtime that the large language model has never seen before.

23
00:01:06.599 --> 00:01:09.202
AI agents, however, are token hungry.

24
00:01:09.536 --> 00:01:13.139
they by nature extract and plan and act

25
00:01:13.406 --> 00:01:16.509
and reflect and iterate over multiple, multiple passes.

26
00:01:17.010 --> 00:01:21.014
So agents use multiple LLM calls throughout the course of their execution.

27
00:01:21.214 --> 00:01:23.917
This consumes more tokens, adds additional latency

28
00:01:24.284 --> 00:01:26.586
and the prompt lengths also grow over time.

29
00:01:27.487 --> 00:01:30.790
And as we can see through this one benchmark produced by TheAgentCompany,

30
00:01:30.790 --> 00:01:33.193
this is a paper that came out in September 2025.

31
00:01:33.593 --> 00:01:36.096
Multiple real-world tasks compared against one another

32
00:01:36.096 --> 00:01:38.098
with different LLMs.

33
00:01:38.431 --> 00:01:41.201
You can see that even in some cases,

34
00:01:41.201 --> 00:01:44.204
an entire execution end-to-end costs up to USD $6.8.

35
00:01:44.804 --> 00:01:47.407
Let's talk about a very common use case for agents.

36
00:01:47.407 --> 00:01:50.210
One where we might see in the real world, like customer support.

37
00:01:50.410 --> 00:01:53.313
So in a customer support scenario, agents are trying

38
00:01:53.313 --> 00:01:55.115
to accelerate the time to resolution

39
00:01:55.115 --> 00:01:57.217
of a customer support ticket or inquiry.

40
00:01:57.417 --> 00:02:00.920
Slow agents negatively impact the end customer user experience.

41
00:02:01.154 --> 00:02:04.357
And customer support agents generate tons of frequently asked questions,

42
00:02:04.357 --> 00:02:06.659
redundant data that adds up over time.

43
00:02:06.659 --> 00:02:10.663
And the RAG operations over that data will drive up infrastructure costs.

44
00:02:10.964 --> 00:02:13.366
So take for example, multiple users asking questions

45
00:02:13.366 --> 00:02:14.767
about how they can get a refund,

46
00:02:14.767 --> 00:02:16.369
or how they can get their money back,

47
00:02:16.369 --> 00:02:18.371
or what the refund policy is.

48
00:02:18.371 --> 00:02:21.674
This agent is having to solve all of these scenarios from scratch.

49
00:02:21.975 --> 00:02:24.177
Let's talk about this classic principle called

50
00:02:24.177 --> 00:02:27.981
caching. The idea is not to repeat yourself on redundant information.

51
00:02:28.281 --> 00:02:31.384
Users might ask multiple questions of agents, and in these cases,

52
00:02:31.384 --> 00:02:33.052
we can check the cache for queries

53
00:02:33.052 --> 00:02:34.988
that have already been answered in the past

54
00:02:35.388 --> 00:02:38.691
and avoid invoking the large language model when we don't have to.

55
00:02:38.992 --> 00:02:41.694
This is a great idea in theory, but the problem is that

56
00:02:41.694 --> 00:02:44.998
a naive exact match caching fails for natural language.

57
00:02:45.098 --> 00:02:47.200
So let's look at this example again. I want my money

58
00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:50.436
back, how do I get a refund? What is your refund policy?

59
00:02:50.637 --> 00:02:53.206
In Traditional Caching, you use exact matches on string data.

60
00:02:53.406 --> 00:02:55.642
The words and tokens and characters have to be

61
00:02:55.642 --> 00:02:58.111
exactly the same in order to get a cache hit.

62
00:02:58.511 --> 00:03:01.214
This yields perfect precision but really poor recall

63
00:03:01.414 --> 00:03:04.250
and in most cases very low cache hit rates for natural language.

64
00:03:04.651 --> 00:03:06.019
So in this scenario, all three of

65
00:03:06.019 --> 00:03:07.420
these questions would be a Cache Miss.

66
00:03:08.021 --> 00:03:09.722
But introducing semantic caching,

67
00:03:10.223 --> 00:03:11.691
This is where we can take

68
00:03:11.691 --> 00:03:13.426
advantage of the meaning of the questions.

69
00:03:13.826 --> 00:03:16.229
And now this brings us with higher recall and

70
00:03:16.229 --> 00:03:18.631
higher cache hit rates, the ability to influence performance.

71
00:03:18.831 --> 00:03:22.135
However, this also introduces the risk of false positives.

72
00:03:22.435 --> 00:03:25.038
In other words, this introduces the opportunity for

73
00:03:25.138 --> 00:03:27.040
the cache to hit on the incorrect thing.

74
00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:30.043
So let's talk about how semantic caching works from scratch.

75
00:03:30.143 --> 00:03:32.812
Step one is we're going to embed the user question.

76
00:03:33.246 --> 00:03:35.748
We're going to convert this user question into a vector

77
00:03:35.748 --> 00:03:38.051
that we can use for semantic search.

78
00:03:38.451 --> 00:03:41.254
Second, we're going to compare the similarity

79
00:03:41.254 --> 00:03:44.757
of the user query to every entry in our semantic cache.

80
00:03:45.258 --> 00:03:47.460
And next, we will classify this result.

81
00:03:47.961 --> 00:03:49.862
If the result is close enough to

82
00:03:49.862 --> 00:03:52.031
the original question in terms of semantic distance,

83
00:03:52.232 --> 00:03:54.734
we can go straight back to the user with the answer

84
00:03:54.734 --> 00:03:56.035
that was stored in the cache.

85
00:03:56.035 --> 00:03:57.537
But if it's a cache miss, then

86
00:03:57.570 --> 00:03:59.672
we need to call our RAG system.

87
00:03:59.772 --> 00:04:02.575
The RAG system involves generating some kind of search

88
00:04:03.076 --> 00:04:05.178
and then some kind of call to an LLM.

89
00:04:05.278 --> 00:04:06.879
And at the very end, we need

90
00:04:06.879 --> 00:04:08.481
to still respond back to the user.

91
00:04:08.581 --> 00:04:11.384
Once we have an answer, we can update our cache

92
00:04:11.484 --> 00:04:12.919
with that question and answer pair to

93
00:04:12.919 --> 00:04:14.387
make sure it's reusable in the future.

94
00:04:14.687 --> 00:04:16.522
Now, the very backbone of semantic

95
00:04:16.522 --> 00:04:18.691
caching is this idea of vector search.

96
00:04:18.791 --> 00:04:21.694
And just as a recap, vectors are lists of numbers.

97
00:04:22.128 --> 00:04:25.732
And these numbers represent data and encode meaning and semantics.

98
00:04:25.932 --> 00:04:28.735
There are many example applications of vector search in the wild

99
00:04:28.735 --> 00:04:31.738
including content discovery, search, recommendation systems,

100
00:04:31.738 --> 00:04:34.240
or even fraud and anomaly detection.

101
00:04:34.774 --> 00:04:38.278
Semantic caching in production introduces new kinds of challenges.

102
00:04:38.578 --> 00:04:40.580
It's more than just vector search.

103
00:04:41.080 --> 00:04:43.383
Here we have to care about the cache effectiveness.

104
00:04:43.983 --> 00:04:45.585
In particular, accuracy.

105
00:04:46.286 --> 00:04:47.954
Are we serving the correct results from

106
00:04:47.954 --> 00:04:49.656
the cache when we get a hit?

107
00:04:49.656 --> 00:04:52.058
Performance. Are we making sure that we actually hit

108
00:04:52.058 --> 00:04:54.460
the cache often enough to get value from it?

109
00:04:54.761 --> 00:04:57.030
And can we actually serve the cache

110
00:04:57.030 --> 00:04:59.265
at scale without impacting round trip latency?

111
00:04:59.766 --> 00:05:02.568
The next area we need to care about is Updatability or extensibility.

112
00:05:02.869 --> 00:05:04.971
Can we refresh and invalidate or warm

113
00:05:04.971 --> 00:05:07.373
the semantic cache as data evolves over time?

114
00:05:07.774 --> 00:05:10.677
And lastly, Observability. Can we make sure to measure and observe

115
00:05:10.877 --> 00:05:13.079
the right metrics like cache hit-rate, latency,

116
00:05:13.413 --> 00:05:15.481
the cost savings and cache quality.

117
00:05:15.481 --> 00:05:16.749
So in this course, we're going

118
00:05:16.749 --> 00:05:18.184
to be focusing on four different metrics

119
00:05:18.184 --> 00:05:20.086
to measure the effectiveness of the cache.

120
00:05:20.086 --> 00:05:22.388
That is Cache Hit Rate, which is the frequency

121
00:05:22.388 --> 00:05:25.591
of times that we hit the cache given a distance threshold.

122
00:05:25.591 --> 00:05:28.594
And this is what primarily influences our cost savings

123
00:05:28.594 --> 00:05:30.797
that we get from adding a semantic cache.

124
00:05:30.797 --> 00:05:33.199
And then the next set of metrics are ranking metrics,

125
00:05:33.199 --> 00:05:36.903
like Precision and Recall and F1 Score. These will be used to

126
00:05:37.136 --> 00:05:39.605
help us understand how accurate our cache is

127
00:05:39.605 --> 00:05:41.341
when we actually get a hit. Now,

128
00:05:41.341 --> 00:05:43.309
this course is also going to help us

129
00:05:43.309 --> 00:05:45.011
introduce different ways to improve

130
00:05:45.011 --> 00:05:47.914
the accuracy and performance of the semantic cache.

131
00:05:48.314 --> 00:05:50.917
For improving the precision and recall of the cache,

132
00:05:50.917 --> 00:05:52.618
we'll focus on a couple different methods

133
00:05:52.919 --> 00:05:54.921
like tuning the distance threshold

134
00:05:55.321 --> 00:05:58.624
and adding additional reranker steps like a cross-encoder model

135
00:05:58.624 --> 00:06:00.626
or a large language model as a judge.

136
00:06:00.860 --> 00:06:02.862
Additionally, if techniques like Fuzzy Matching

137
00:06:02.862 --> 00:06:05.031
can be used to help us handle typos

138
00:06:05.031 --> 00:06:07.033
and exact match cases

139
00:06:07.033 --> 00:06:10.636
before even invoking the embeddings associated with the cache.

140
00:06:10.636 --> 00:06:12.338
This helps us save on compute.

141
00:06:12.505 --> 00:06:14.907
And lastly, additional filters for different kinds

142
00:06:14.907 --> 00:06:17.310
of data and context like temporal data,

143
00:06:17.510 --> 00:06:20.313
so time sensitive queries or Code Detection,

144
00:06:20.513 --> 00:06:24.317
like domain specific code, Python code, Java code, you name it.

145
00:06:24.517 --> 00:06:26.753
All of those things that should potentially bypass

146
00:06:26.753 --> 00:06:29.021
any kind of caching operation to begin with.

147
00:06:29.489 --> 00:06:32.592
In this course, we're primarily going to implement a cache

148
00:06:32.592 --> 00:06:35.595
that uses techniques to improve precision and recall and efficiency.

149
00:06:35.895 --> 00:06:37.730
So let's talk about a real-world

150
00:06:37.730 --> 00:06:39.899
example at a large retailer like Walmart.

151
00:06:40.066 --> 00:06:42.068
Walmart published a paper recently where they

152
00:06:42.068 --> 00:06:44.370
talked about the need for semantic caching

153
00:06:44.370 --> 00:06:47.573
in both internal use cases and their external

154
00:06:47.573 --> 00:06:50.777
customer support use cases. They called this waLLMartCache.

155
00:06:50.877 --> 00:06:53.446
In this cache, they introduced several different techniques

156
00:06:53.446 --> 00:06:55.982
like distribution of the cache across multiple nodes.

157
00:06:56.416 --> 00:07:00.420
adding a flexible decision engine and even pre-loading common FAQs.

158
00:07:00.520 --> 00:07:04.424
And this helps to increase the overall accuracy to nearly 90%.

159
00:07:04.724 --> 00:07:06.526
So let's take a quick dive into

160
00:07:06.526 --> 00:07:08.628
how exactly they got to this particular benchmark.

161
00:07:09.228 --> 00:07:12.899
The first place that we see the contributions from the waLLMartCache

162
00:07:12.899 --> 00:07:15.034
was first by adding a Load Balancer. And

163
00:07:15.034 --> 00:07:17.403
this Load Balancer allows them to grow the system

164
00:07:17.403 --> 00:07:20.506
very easily horizontally, meaning they can add additional

165
00:07:20.540 --> 00:07:23.342
nodes of compute and the cache can scale up

166
00:07:23.342 --> 00:07:25.545
in terms of the number of operations that it can serve.

167
00:07:25.545 --> 00:07:29.048
This is absolutely essential for an organization like Walmart

168
00:07:29.048 --> 00:07:31.050
at scale across the entire world.

169
00:07:31.250 --> 00:07:34.554
Secondly, they added Dual-tiered storage layer for the cache.

170
00:07:34.754 --> 00:07:37.557
By Dual-tiered we mean there's both an L1 and an L2

171
00:07:37.557 --> 00:07:41.360
layer of storage. In L1, this is a Vector Database.

172
00:07:41.360 --> 00:07:44.363
This is a simple retrieval based on semantic search.

173
00:07:44.697 --> 00:07:47.300
to find similar entries in the semantic cache.

174
00:07:47.567 --> 00:07:50.002
And L2, this is an in-memory cache like Redis

175
00:07:50.269 --> 00:07:52.572
or some other database to just do a simple lookup.

176
00:07:52.872 --> 00:07:55.875
Given the IDs that come from the L1 cache, we go find

177
00:07:56.375 --> 00:07:59.679
their data and metadata in the L2 cache for retrieval.

178
00:08:00.079 --> 00:08:04.484
Lastly is Multi-tenancy. So given the size of an organization like Walmart,

179
00:08:04.584 --> 00:08:06.486
they can have multiple teams, multiple tenants,

180
00:08:06.586 --> 00:08:09.355
and applications use the same storage infrastructure.

181
00:08:09.755 --> 00:08:12.725
by serving multiple tenants out of the same cache storage.

182
00:08:13.025 --> 00:08:15.027
And next, Walmart added a Decision Engine.

183
00:08:15.228 --> 00:08:18.231
The Decision Engine is there to improve the cache precision

184
00:08:18.231 --> 00:08:20.032
beyond pure semantic search.

185
00:08:20.433 --> 00:08:22.935
In their particular decision engine they added a module

186
00:08:22.935 --> 00:08:26.539
for Code Detection. and also Temporal Context Detection.

187
00:08:27.240 --> 00:08:30.042
In any case, a user query that comes in involves code

188
00:08:30.343 --> 00:08:32.044
or time-sensitive queries.

189
00:08:32.278 --> 00:08:34.780
These avoid the cache operations altogether

190
00:08:34.780 --> 00:08:37.783
and just go straight through the traditional LLM or RAG based workflow.

191
00:08:38.084 --> 00:08:41.053
And these are both added before semantic search happens.

192
00:08:41.454 --> 00:08:42.755
So in this course, we're going

193
00:08:42.755 --> 00:08:44.257
to end with building an AI agent

194
00:08:44.257 --> 00:08:46.759
from scratch using a LangGraph workflow.

195
00:08:47.059 --> 00:08:50.763
In this LangGraph workflow, we will take a large, complicated user query,

196
00:08:50.763 --> 00:08:52.765
like from a customer support use case.

197
00:08:52.865 --> 00:08:55.668
And the agent will decompose that query into smaller pieces.

198
00:08:55.768 --> 00:08:57.770
The agent will then check the cache.

199
00:08:57.837 --> 00:08:59.305
for each query to find if

200
00:08:59.305 --> 00:09:01.007
we've already answered this in the past.

201
00:09:01.007 --> 00:09:04.010
And if we haven't, the agent will proceed with additional research

202
00:09:04.010 --> 00:09:06.312
and evaluation iterations

203
00:09:06.312 --> 00:09:08.114
to make sure we get quality answers.

204
00:09:08.114 --> 00:09:10.616
And at the very end, an LLM will synthesize

205
00:09:10.616 --> 00:09:13.819
the results back to the user in a personalized fashion.

206
00:09:13.819 --> 00:09:17.623
This will be paired with a front-end experience that we provide

207
00:09:17.623 --> 00:09:20.126
that allows you to put the URL of your favorite website.

208
00:09:20.126 --> 00:09:23.029
We can scrape this website for all of its raw content.

209
00:09:23.362 --> 00:09:25.565
enabling you to chat with the data.

210
00:09:25.865 --> 00:09:28.868
By the end, we will build up a Semantic Cache from scratch

211
00:09:29.168 --> 00:09:32.872
that is filled with commonly asked questions from your testing.

212
00:09:33.172 --> 00:09:36.676
And this agent will be able to leverage those Semantic Cache entries

213
00:09:36.976 --> 00:09:38.578
for improved performance over time.

214
00:09:38.678 --> 00:09:41.180
In the very next lesson, we're first going to start

215
00:09:41.280 --> 00:09:43.182
with building a Semantic Cache from scratch.

216
00:09:43.583 --> 00:09:45.451
Let's get started.
